MAS	-	Module	Activation	System
ARCOS	-	Specification,	Testing	&	Governance	Framework
Status:	Research,	not	implemented.	This	document	describes
the	intended	coordination	layer.	It	has	been	reasoned	through
and	revised	across	several	rounds	of	internal	review,	but	it	has
not	been	built	as	live	infrastructure	-	it	exists	today	only	as	an
off-chain	TypeScript	orchestrator	inside	the	v0.5	prototype,	with
no	deployed	on-chain	enforcement.	Treat	every	mechanism
below	as	a	design	proposal,	not	a	working	system.
What	MAS	Is
MAS	(Module	Activation	System)	is	the	only	coordination	layer
between	Modules.
No	Module	ever	calls	another	Module	directly.	Every	interaction
between	Modules	-	a	Transfer	checking	Ownership,	a	Permission
lookup,	an	aggregation	across	several	Modules	at	once	-	happens
through	MAS.
MAS	is	what	makes	independently	governed	Modules	composable
instead	of	each	one	having	to	hand-wire	calls	into	every	other
Module	it	might	interact	with.
What	MAS	Actually	Does
Routing	-	MAS	is	the	only	entry	point	into	a	Module’s	state	or
logic.
Aggregation	-	when	an	operation	needs	input	from	more	than	one
Module,	MAS	collects	each	one’s	check	and	returns	a	single
decision.	No	Module	needs	to	know	the	others	exist.
No	duplicate	state	-	if	one	Module’s	state	answers	a	question
another	Module	needs,	the	second	queries	the	first	through	MAS
instead	of	holding	its	own	copy	of	that	state.
How	MAS	Connects	to	Native
Enforcement
Native	Enforcement	does	not	decide	anything	itself	-	the	on-chain
contract	only	verifies	a	signed	decision.
MAS	is	what	computes	that	decision.	Native	Enforcement’s	entire
guarantee	is	only	as	strong	as	what	MAS	decides	and	signs.
Today:	MAS	runs	off-chain,	in	TypeScript,	operated	entirely	by
ARCOS.	There	is	no	deployed	on-chain	enforcement	contract	yet,
and	no	independent	party	checking	MAS’s	decisions.
Who	Runs	MAS,	Today
ARCOS,	alone.	This	should	be	stated	plainly	wherever	MAS	is
described,	not	left	implicit.
The	honest	current	claim	is:	a	well-governed,	permissioned
service	-	not	trustless	infrastructure.	That	becomes	true	only	if
and	when	MAS’s	decision-making	is	no	longer	solely	ARCOS’s	to
make.
The	Real,	Cautionary	Precedent
OpenSea	built	a	similar	centralized-checker	pattern	for	royalty
enforcement	-	the	Operator	Filter	Registry	-	and	deprecated	it	in
2023.
The	failure	that	killed	it:	a	legitimate	token	owner	could	be	blocked
from	moving	their	own	token	indefinitely,	with	no	override,
because	one	centralized	authority	made	the	call	and	there	was	no
way	around	a	bad	or	stuck	decision.
MAS,	as	currently	designed,	has	the	identical	shape:	a	centralized
decision-maker	whose	signature	gates	whether	a	transfer	or
operation	completes.	The	risk	is	not	hypothetical	-	it	has	already
played	out	once	in	a	closely	comparable	system.
The	Two	Separate	Problems,	and
Why	They	Need	Different	Fixes
These	get	conflated	easily,	and	they	need	different	solutions:
1.	Who	decides?	-	currently	ARCOS	alone.	Decentralizing	this	is	a
real,	valuable,	and	expensive	direction	-	options	range	from	a
federated	multi-signer	set	(a	fixed	group	of	known	operators,
majority-signature	required)	to	a	staked	validator	set	(closer	to
running	a	small	consensus	system,	comparable	in	cost	to	the
“ARCOS	chain”	proposal).	This	is	credible	future	work,	not	a	near
term	fix.
2.	What	happens	when	the	decider	is	wrong,	unavailable,	or
contested?	-	this	is	currently	unsolved	regardless	of	who’s
deciding.	A	federated	MAS	with	no	escape	hatch	has	the	exact
same	failure	mode	as	a	centralized	one,	just	voted	on	by	more
people.	This	is	the	part	that	actually	caused	OpenSea’s	system	to
fail,	and	it	is	fixable	now,	independent	of	decentralization.
What	an	Escape	Hatch	Needs	to
Guarantee
Not	yet	designed,	but	the	requirement	is	clear	from	the	OpenSea
precedent:	a	legitimate	owner	must	never	be	permanently	blocked
because	MAS	is	unavailable,	wrong,	or	unresponsive.	Candidate
mechanisms,	none	yet	chosen:
A	timeout:	if	MAS	does	not	respond	within	a	defined	window,	the
operation	falls	back	to	an	unrestricted	native	transfer.
An	on-chain	override	path,	usable	under	narrowly	defined
conditions.
A	defined	“MAS	unavailable”	state	that	the	contract	can	detect	and
act	on	independently.
Open,	Unresolved	Points
Whether	Transfer’s	decision	draws	on	all	Modules	unconditionally,
or	only	Modules	with	a	transfer-relevant	stake,	is	undecided	-	this
affects	both	the	gas-cost	model	(Native-Enforcement.md)	and	how
much	surface	area	a	MAS	failure	has.
No	escape-hatch	mechanism	has	been	designed	or	chosen	yet.
No	path	to	decentralizing	MAS’s	decision-making	has	been
specified	beyond	naming	federated	multi-sig	as	the	more	credible
near-term	option	over	full	validator	decentralization.
Versioning	and	authentication	of	MAS’s	decisions	against	an
immutable	on-chain	contract,	as	the	off-chain	logic	evolves,	is	an
open	problem
